---
const faqs = [
	{
		q: 'What is CairnCMS?',
		a: [
			'CairnCMS is an open-source platform for managing content and data in your own SQL database. It gives your team an admin app, generated REST and GraphQL APIs, permissions, automation, and realtime updates, while your data stays in standard tables you control. It\'s licensed under GPLv3 and built for teams that run their own infrastructure.'
		],
	},
	{
		q: 'Is CairnCMS really a CMS?',
		a: [
			'CairnCMS is not a traditional CMS in that it isn\'t built around a fixed content model.',
			'Instead, your SQL schema defines the model. CairnCMS then turns that model into a governed workspace with APIs, permissions, and workflows for managing structured content and operational data across many digital surfaces: websites, applications, dashboards, or internal tools, for example.',
			'The "CMS" in CairnCMS reflects the modern role of content management, giving teams a governed workspace for the data and content their systems depend on.'
		],
	},
	{
		q: 'What do you mean by "content and data infrastructure"?',
		a: [
			'CairnCMS is infrastructure in the application sense.',
			'It does not replace your database, storage provider, or deployment platform. CairnCMS is an application layer on top of your SQL database and file storage backend. It adds the workspace, generated APIs, permissions, file management, dashboards, automation, and extension points needed to manage and govern the content and data your systems depend on.',
			'Your database remains the source of truth. CairnCMS can read an existing schema or help you create a new one, but your application data stays in standard SQL tables you can inspect, query, back up, migrate, and use directly.',
			'That is why CairnCMS is described as content and data infrastructure: it provides the operating layer around your data, while leaving you in control of the database, storage, and deployment model.'
		],
	},
	{
		q: 'Can I use CairnCMS for production systems?',
		a: [
			'Yes. CairnCMS is intended for production self-hosted deployments, including larger and more complex systems, not just prototypes or demos.',
			'Whether it is the right fit depends on your requirements, operating model, and comfort running the stack yourself, but the project is not positioned as a temporary or limited-use tool.',
		],
	},
	{
		q: 'Can I run more than one CairnCMS server?',
		a: [
			'Yes. Run as many API servers as you need against the same database, connected through Redis. Scheduled automations and realtime updates coordinate across all of them.',
		],
	},
	{
		q: 'What databases does CairnCMS support?',
		a: [
			'PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB, and SQLite. Releases are tested against all four, including older PostgreSQL 10 and MySQL 5 versions.',
			'Code for the commercial databases CockroachDB, Microsoft SQL Server, and Oracle is still present but untested, and support for them will eventually be deprecated. Treat them as unsupported, and validate them yourself if you depend on one.',
		],
	},
	{
		q: 'How can I contribute to CairnCMS?',
		a: [
			'The best way to support CairnCMS is to use it, file issues when you find bugs, contribute fixes when you can, and share the project with people who might benefit from it.',
			'Contributions go through the standard GitHub pull request workflow with the Developer Certificate of Origin sign-off requirement. The contributing guide has details. The AI policy explains the project’s expectations around AI-assisted contributions.',
			'For larger changes, file an issue first to discuss the approach before investing significant time in implementation.',
		],
	},
];
---

<section id="faq" class="section-pad section-border-top textured-section">
	<div class="speckle-texture" aria-hidden="true"></div>
	<div class="container">
		<div class="row justify-content-center">
			<div class="col-lg-8 text-center">
				<span class="eyebrow">FAQ</span>
				<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
				<p class="lead mt-3 mb-5">Common questions about CairnCMS.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="row justify-content-center">
			<div class="col-lg-8">
				<div class="faq-accordion">
					{
						faqs.map((item) => (
							<details class="faq-item">
								<summary class="faq-question">{item.q}</summary>
								<div class="faq-answer">
									{item.a.map((paragraph) => (
										<p>{paragraph}</p>
									))}
								</div>
							</details>
						))
					}
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>
